<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bani Thani</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bani Thani is a painting of a woman in profile made at Kishangarh in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> around 1750, usually attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nihal-chand">Nihal Chand</a>. Its elongated face, arched eyebrows and lotus-shaped eyes became the hallmark of the Kishangarh school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-miniature">miniature painting</a>. The woman is traditionally identified with a singer and poet at the court of Raja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sawant-singh">Sawant Singh</a>, and the image has been called the Mona Lisa of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bani-thani.jpg" alt="Bani Thani" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eugene a · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:4_Radha_(Bani_Thani),_Kishangarh,_ca._1750,_National_Museum_New_Delhi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The woman known as Bani Thani was born Vishnupriya and was a singer employed by the stepmother of Raja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sawant-singh">Sawant Singh</a> of Kishangarh, who reigned from 1748 to 1764. Drawn by her beauty and singing, he made her his mistress and later one of his wives. She took the name Bani Thani, &quot;the decked out lady&quot;, from the jewellery and make-up she wore as queen. Both wrote poetry, he as Nagari Das and she as Rasikbihari, and they shared a devotion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>. After he abdicated they retired to Vrindavan; both died in the 1760s and have twin chhatris near the Nagari Kunj temple.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sawant-singh">Sawant Singh</a> had his artists depict the couple's love in the manner of that of Radha and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>. A group of paintings of about 1750, attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nihal-chand">Nihal Chand</a>, shows Radha and Krishna with the same models, assumed to be the raja and Bani Thani. Her stylised features, with arched eyebrows, long lotus-like eyes and a pointed chin, recall Indian sculpture. The Kishangarh school took lush vegetation, night skies and movement from Būndī painting and the profile portrait from Mughal painting, but is set apart by meticulous detail, rich colour and fine technique.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sawant-singh">Sawant Singh</a> belonged to the Vallabhācārya sect devoted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>, religious themes flourished at his court, which may explain why the portrait of the queen was compared to Radha and is believed to be inspired by her. In the Ashta Nayika classification of heroines, Bani Thani is identified as the Vasakasajja nayika, with Sringara rasa, romantic love, predominating. Her idealised face became the standard of beauty in later Kishangarh painting, and one painting of her appeared on an Indian stamp issued on 5 May 1973.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bani Thani” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The court became a centre of Krishna devotion and art: under Raja Sawant Singh, the poet Nagari Das, painters such as Nihal Chand created the elongated, lyrical Kishangarh style, seen above all in the portrait known as <b>Bani Thani</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kishangarh-state">Kishangarh State</a></li></ul></section>
